  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nUpper ridging over the western and central portions of the U.S.\nextends into eastern Montana through the beginning of the week,\ncontinuing the warm, but seasonally average, temperatures. Given \nNE Montana will be on the northeast quadrant of the ridge under \nnorthwest flow aloft, weak embedded shortwaves may be enough to \nset off a few thunderstorms across far northeast counties this \nevening and tomorrow afternoon. While any storms will remain \nfairly isolated, there remains potential for a stronger thunderstorm\nto be possible.\n\nFor Wednesday into Friday, upper ridging amplifies as the ridge \naxis pushes across central Montana and adjacent Canada with a \nwarming trend resuming. Conditions will largely be dry save for an\nisolated thunderstorm with the diurnal heating and instability, \nand weak embedded disturbances. Most places however will remain \ndry through this period. As of now monsoonal moisture looks to \nremain south of the region, giving an overall drier setup.\n\nThe ridge axis will begin to slide downstream on Friday into the\nweekend, lending to a return to triple digit heat across the area\nas well as a continuation of thunderstorm chances at times. If\nthe current ensemble means hold, eventual heat related products\nwill be necessary.\n\nHonorable forecast mention, so far with little impact, is the \nwildfire smoke. Haze will be around at times through much of the \nupcoming week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nIncreased PoPs with blended CAMs for afternoon convection.\nEnsemble consensus is fairly solid depicting upper ridging over \nthe foreseeable future. No changes to NBM otherwise.",
